VTOP @VERSION@ for OpenVMS (vms-vtop, @ARCH@) - EARLY TEST RELEASE
==================================================================

VTOP is a full-screen, live system monitor for OpenVMS, inspired by btop: CPU
(by processor mode and per CPU), memory, disks and network panels with history
graphs, a process list you can sort, filter, view as a tree and act on, and a
panel for what only VMS has: the cluster, shadow sets and queues.

Starting it
-----------
    $ @VTOP$ROOT:[000000]VTOP$SETUP.COM   ! once, or in LOGIN.COM / SYLOGIN.COM
    $ VTOP
    $ VTOP/INTERVAL=5                    ! seconds between samples, 1 to 10
    $ VTOP/GRAPH=VT/COLOUR=MONO          ! a monochrome VT220/VT320/VT520

It needs a terminal of at least 70 x 20 and uses all of it (resize as you like).
On an xterm-type terminal with UTF-8 it draws braille graphs in 24-bit colour;
on a VT or DECterm, DEC line drawing in 8 colours. /GRAPH=BRAILLE|BLOCK|VT and
/COLOUR=24|256|8|MONO choose for yourself. h, ? or Help shows every key; q, F10
or Ctrl/Z leaves.

Keys
----
+ -        refresh interval            g c     graph characters, colours
v          net, vms or both panels     b n     the network device shown
Up Down Prev Next   select a process   < >     sort column; r reverses it
t          tree by owner process       / f     filter: name, user, image, PID
i Return   details: image, login time, quotas, privileges
S R        suspend, resume             D P     delete, set priority
w          save these settings         Ctrl/W  measure the terminal, redraw

S, R, D and P are capitals, so a lowercase slip does nothing. Each asks first,
naming the PID, process, user and image; only Y says yes, and keys typed ahead
are thrown away before the question.

Privileges
----------
VTOP enables no privilege. It shows the processes your current privileges let
you see (all of them with WORLD, your group's with GROUP, else your own; the
proc panel's title says which), and VMS checks each action exactly as it would
SET PROCESS/SUSPEND or STOP/IDENTIFICATION. VTOP won't suspend or delete itself.

The vms panel
-------------
The cluster's members with their votes, and the quorum; each shadow set, with
copy or merge progress; every queue with its state, running jobs and limit,
pending and holding jobs (holding includes /AFTER jobs). Stopped, stalled and
paused queues come first, in warning colour. Page and swap files are the
totals in the mem panel (per-file figures need privilege).

Options file
------------
w writes the interval, sort, tree, panel and network device (and the graph and
colour, if you chose them this time) to SYS$LOGIN:VTOP.INI, a new version each
time; VTOP reads it when it starts. Lines are "name = value"; ! starts a
comment. The qualifiers and logical names below win over the file.

Logical names
-------------
VTOP$GRAPH     BRAILLE, BLOCK or VT, as /GRAPH
VTOP$COLOUR    24, 256, 8 or MONO, as /COLOUR
VTOP$OPTIONS   the options file (default SYS$LOGIN:VTOP.INI)
VTOP$SIZE      "132x40": for a terminal that doesn't answer a size query

Setting it up
-------------
PRODUCT INSTALL has defined VTOP$ROOT and VTOP once. To define them at every
boot, add to SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM:
    $ @SYS$STARTUP:VTOP$STARTUP.COM
and give users the VTOP command (SYLOGIN.COM or their LOGIN.COM):
    $ @VTOP$ROOT:[000000]VTOP$SETUP.COM

Limits of this release
----------------------
- Disks show operations per second, not bytes: VMS counts operations per
  device.
- On IA64, VTOP's own CPU time may show as nothing: short bursts fall between
  the clock ticks that charge it.

VTOP is unrelated to btop and contains none of its code or themes.
